{#
  The account-security message, as an ordered parameter list.

  A separate descriptor rather than a flag on `notification`, and the difference
  is one parameter that is deliberately absent: THERE IS NO LINK. There is nothing
  to open — an account activation and a password reset both send the reader to
  sign in, not to a resource — and a link in a WhatsApp message about a password
  is precisely the shape of a phishing message. That matters more here than in an
  email, because the sender is a phone number the reader may not recognise, so the
  one thing this message must not do is ask them to tap something.

  What it carries instead is the "if you did not do this" line, which is what makes
  the notification actionable without a link.

  See `notification.v1.params.j2` for the rendering rules; they are identical.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE APPROVED TEMPLATE THIS MATCHES
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Register a template named `security`, category UTILITY, in each language the
  deployment sends (fr, ar, en_US), with a body of exactly four variables:

      {{1}}

      *{{2}}*
      {{3}}

      {{4}}

  {{1}} greeting       — "Bonjour Amina"
  {{2}} title          — the notification's heading, from core/notifications.py
  {{3}} message        — the notification's sentence, same source
  {{4}} security note  — "If you did not do this, contact your administrator"

  The count and the order below ARE the contract with that template.
#}
{{ chrome.greeting }}
{{ title }}
{{ message }}
{{ chrome.security_note }}
